* cpu1: what one conversion costs the node it is taken from.
*
* The converter is a switch onto a 4 pF sampling capacitor through 50 ohms of
* its own - ST's Table 84. When the switch closes, that capacitor takes its
* share of the charge on the 10 nF at the pin, and the series resistor has one
* sampling window to put it back.
*
* `checks/test_adc.py` works this out as charge sharing followed by a single
* exponential, and deliberately does not use ST's Equation 1 - which asks a
* different question and rejects every value that works here. This deck is the
* check on that reasoning: the switch and the converter's own resistance are in
* the circuit rather than assumed away.
*
* The worst case throughout: the smallest capacitor, the largest source
* impedance, the largest sampling capacitor, and the shortest window.

Vin in 0 DC 1
Rsource in src @header.source_impedance:max@
Rseries src pin @adc.fast1.series.resistance:max@
Cshunt pin 0 @adc.fast1.shunt.capacitance:min@

.model sampler SW(vt=0.5 vh=0 ron=1m roff=1g)
Vgate gate 0 PWL(0 0 0.1n 1)
Ssample pin adc gate 0 sampler
Radc adc hold @mcu.adc_sample_resistance:nom@
Cadc hold 0 @mcu.adc_sample_capacitance:max@

* The previous conversion left the sampling capacitor at zero and this one is at
* full scale, which is the most charge the sharing can move.
.ic v(pin)=1 v(hold)=0

.tran 0.1n @adc.sampling_time:min@ uic

* What the converter is left holding when the window closes, and the same thing
* as an error against full scale in LSB.
.meas tran v_held FIND v(hold) AT=@adc.sampling_time:min@
.meas tran settling_error_lsb PARAM='(1-v_held)*4096'

* The node immediately after the sharing, before the resistor has put anything
* back. This is the number the analytic check starts from.
.meas tran v_pin_after_sharing FIND v(pin) AT=1n

.end
